@page "/layout"

<PageTitle>Layout · Bmotion samples</PageTitle>

<h1>Layout</h1>

<p>
    A re-render that moves an element is a jump: Blazor writes the new markup and the browser lays it out at once.
    <code>Layout</code> turns the jump into motion - measure before the render, measure after it, and play the difference
    (FLIP). @LayoutNote
</p>

<section>
    <h2>Moving with the list</h2>
    <p>
        Each tile is a <code>&lt;Bmotion Layout="true"&gt;</code> with a <code>@@key</code>, so Blazor moves the same
        element rather than rewriting another one, and Bmotion slides it from where it was. Nothing here animates a
        position by hand: the list changes, and the grid lays itself out. Measuring costs a reflow (and on Server holds
        the element for a round trip), so the tiles measure only when <code>LayoutDependency</code> - bumped by Shuffle
        and Sort - changes, not on every render of the page.
    </p>

    <div class="toolbar">
        <button type="button" id="shuffle" @onclick="Shuffle">Shuffle</button>
        <button type="button" id="sort" @onclick="Sort">Sort</button>
    </div>

    <div class="number-grid">
        @foreach (var number in _numbers)
        {
            <Bmotion @key="number" Layout="true" LayoutDependency="_arrangement" Transition="Bm.Tween(0.4, BmEase.InOut)">
                <div class="number-tile tone-@(number % 4)">@number</div>
            </Bmotion>
        }
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>One underline, many tabs</h2>
    <p>
        Only the selected tab renders an underline, so choosing another removes one element and adds another.
        Because both have <code>LayoutId="tab-underline"</code>, the new one starts from the old one's box and travels
        to its own: one underline, as far as anyone can tell.
    </p>

    <div class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Seasons">
        @foreach (var tab in Tabs)
        {
            <button type="button" role="tab" class="tab" aria-selected="@(tab == _tab ? "true" : "false")" @onclick="() => _tab = tab">
                @tab
                @if (tab == _tab)
                {
                    <Bmotion LayoutId="tab-underline" Layout="true" Transition="Bm.Tween(0.3, BmEase.InOut)">
                        <span class="underline"></span>
                    </Bmotion>
                }
            </button>
        }
    </div>
    <p class="tab-panel" role="tabpanel">@TabText(_tab)</p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Drag to reorder</h2>
    <p>
        <code>BmotionReorderGroup</code> makes every row draggable by its grip; the others make way as it passes, and the
        new order is written to the bound list on release. From the keyboard, <kbd>Tab</kbd> to a row,
        <kbd>Space</kbd> picks it up, the arrow keys move it, <kbd>Space</kbd> drops it and <kbd>Escape</kbd> puts it
        back. A new order from anywhere else - Reverse here - animates the same way.
    </p>

    <div class="reorder-list">
        <BmotionReorderGroup @bind-Items="_tasks" HandleSelector=".grip" Context="task" OnReorder="() => _reorders++">
            <div class="reorder-row">
                <span class="grip" aria-hidden="true">⠿</span>
                <span>@task</span>
            </div>
        </BmotionReorderGroup>
    </div>

    <div class="toolbar">
        <button type="button" id="reverse" @onclick="Reverse">Reverse</button>
        <span class="muted">OnReorder: <span id="reorders">@_reorders</span></span>
    </div>
    <p class="muted" id="order">@string.Join(" → ", _tasks)</p>
</section>
